WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.460
Everybody is just rushing
forward headlessly.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.450
Where are the NCOs?

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:12.690
Where are the officers guiding
them, controlling the units,

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.640
doing their best to keep people together?

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.942
I'm Alexander Watson.
I'm professor of history

00:00:16.942 --> 00:00:19.020
at Goldsmiths, University of London.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.610
I'm an expert on the First World War.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:21.810
I've written three books.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.090
And today we're going to
be looking at battle scenes

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:27.274
in First World War movies
to judge how real they are.

00:00:34.085 --> 00:00:37.620
In August 1914, cavalry plays
quite an important role.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.030
We tend to think of the First World War

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:42.150
as being entirely static, but
it did start off very mobile.

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.060
The Germans storm through Belgium,

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.477
and they occupy a large
chunk of northeastern France.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.400
Some of the cavalry was
equipped with swords.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.800
This was one of the things
that they were trained to do.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.160
Key omission from how
these British cavalrymen

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.650
are presented is that
none of them have rifles.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:02.610
And that's a big mistake.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.250
Because while British cavalry
were trained to charge

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.840
like they're portrayed doing here,

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:08.370
they were also trained to scout,

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.770
and they were also trained
to act as mounted infantry.

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:12.492
So they would dismount,

00:01:12.492 --> 00:01:14.820
and they would be able
to defend a position

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.770
or work around to the flank of a position.

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.200
So they've got multiple roles,

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.970
and that was actually
what made them so useful.

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:25.054
So this idea that the film is
going to hammer into our heads

00:01:25.054 --> 00:01:28.625
that cavalry are obsolete at this point

00:01:28.625 --> 00:01:30.625
is actually not true.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:38.093
All right, so this is the point

00:01:38.093 --> 00:01:39.270
that everything gets ridiculous.

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.190
What they have done is they've
taken their machine guns

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:43.530
and they've put them in a
forest all aimed at their camp.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.090
And there are six machine guns here.

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:47.700
Six machine guns was the allowance

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.400
for an entire German regiment in 1914.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.620
German regiment was around 3,000 men.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.570
So this is the number of machine
guns that would be expected

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:56.490
to support 3,000 men.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:02:01.080
In reality, in 1914, you
had about seven machine guns

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:03.510
for every 2 kilometers of ground.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.880
You wouldn't have
arranged six machine guns

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:07.200
behind three trees.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.183
This is the literal
definition of overkill.

00:02:15.966 --> 00:02:17.291
As a cavalryman, if you're confronted

00:02:17.291 --> 00:02:19.490
by an entire regiment's
worth of machine guns,

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:21.720
or even just one, which
would be sufficient,

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.583
why would you keep on charging towards it?

00:02:23.583 --> 00:02:25.530
That makes no sense.

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:28.740
What you would do is you
would veer off to the left

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:29.880
or to the right.

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.010
You would get into cover.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:33.510
There's plenty of forest here.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:36.300
And then you would use your
training as mounted infantry

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:38.198
to dismount and try and take

00:02:38.198 --> 00:02:39.506
these machine guns from the flank.

00:02:39.506 --> 00:02:41.940
Also, as well, cavalry in a forest,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.620
if you charge into a forest on a horse,

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.030
you're going to break the horse's legs.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.268
This is a really, really bad idea.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:49.783
Three out of 10?

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:57.390
This is a great opening.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.060
It's really good to see the smoke

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.590
that they've got in no man's land.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:02.760
This is 1916.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:04.620
The French infantry
are going in to attack.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:07.650
In order to screen their infantry

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:11.370
from machine-gun fire, rifle fire,

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:12.900
the French would lay smoke.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.720
They'd put smoke shells into no man's land

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.700
so that their troops could come forward

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.850
without easily being seen and
without easily being aimed at.

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:28.410
And we've got an officer who
is actually leading the men.

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:29.850
This guy is desperately trying

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.130
to maintain control of
his troops, to guide them,

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:33.960
to show which direction they need to go.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:34.793
It's great to see.

00:03:34.793 --> 00:03:37.135
It's so often missed from
First World War films.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.000
So, the uniforms in this
scene are very authentic.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.990
And what he's wearing on his
head is an Adrian helmet.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:51.000
The French army was
the first of the armies

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.840
to introduce proper head
protection for its troops.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.910
And that was because head injuries

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:59.550
in the first year of
the war were a really,

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:02.040
really serious strain on manpower.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.548
And so having this head protection

00:04:03.548 --> 00:04:05.940
lowered injuries, lowered fatalities.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.110
It's a pretty light helmet.

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:10.110
A bullet would penetrate it.
But it would protect soldiers.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:12.330
It could protect soldiers from shrapnel.

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:14.160
This is an Adrian helmet
from the First World War.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.870
This is a medical corps helmet.

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.670
The badges on the front were different

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.365
depending on what arm
the soldier belonged to.

00:04:20.365 --> 00:04:21.600
You can see this is a medical corps one

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.670
because it's got a
snake and a staff on it.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:25.050
Key thing is the ridge.

00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.990
The ridge, it turns out, was really useful

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:32.410
for protecting against
the airburst from shells,

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:33.840
for, like, concussion.

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:34.847
I think it was deliberate.

00:04:34.847 --> 00:04:37.290
I think they were basing it
on a fire brigade helmet,

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.032
but it turned out really, really well.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.227
So, this attack is
realistically portrayed too.

00:04:47.227 --> 00:04:49.680
The men are trying to keep spaced out.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.660
And that was really, really important.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:53.520
All armies told men,

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.670
when you go forward, you need
to keep space between you,

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.070
because if you clumped together,

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:01.530
then you presented a perfect
target for a machine gun.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.830
So keeping spaced out was a
really key aspect of survival.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:07.410
The other thing that you see
these French soldiers doing

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.600
is using the terrain for cover.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:11.220
They're going in and out of the dips

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:12.570
in order to move forward

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:14.730
and protect themselves
from machine-gun fire

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:17.220
and the really
violent-looking artillery fire

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:18.690
that's coming down on them.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.700
The one thing that it misses,

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.360
it's got the French infantry
all armed with rifles.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.400
And by this point in the war, by mid-1916,

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.800
the French had already
developed a light machine gun.

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.258
I'd give this an eight out of 10.

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.320
We don't think of watches
as weapons of war,

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.140
but they really, really
were in the First World War.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.020
If you were going to have a
successful attack by 1917,

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.970
everything had to be synchronized.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.370
The first wave needed to go over together,

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.350
followed then afterwards
by the second wave.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.240
But above all, you needed to synchronize

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.250
with the artillery behind you.

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.950
That's something that a
lot of filmmakers forget,

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.170
that infantry didn't
simply act on their own.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:01.260
They acted in combined arms
with an artillery bombardment.

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.470
So getting that timing right
was really, really crucial.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.270
And we see that with the
officer looking at his watch.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.653
That's a great start to the scene.

00:06:09.831 --> 00:06:11.567
Officer: You can't possibly
make it that way, mate.

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:13.067
Are you bloody insane?

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.290
There is some historical
context to this scene.

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.480
The battle of the Somme, huge battle.

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.290
Half a million casualties on both sides.

00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:24.700
Took place in the second half of 1916.

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.720
And the Germans really suffered badly.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.660
And they decided to withdraw their lines

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:33.780
back to stronger positions in early 1917.

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:35.490
And the backstory here

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:38.460
is that the British army is pursuing them.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:39.960
This is a temporary trench,

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.120
so that's why there aren't any sandbags.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:45.570
It's simply been dug to
protect the assaulting troops.

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:47.040
This is meant to take place on the Somme

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.600
or just beyond the Somme battlefield.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.000
And the ground there was really chalky.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:52.230
It was known to be white and chalky,

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:54.690
and they've really represented
that well in the film.

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:56.357
Officer: No, no, no, no!

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:03.660
I never understand why
he goes over the parapet.

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:04.800
In a First World War trench,

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:06.360
you've got kind of two mounds of earth,

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:07.560
one behind it and one in front of it.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:09.000
The one in front of
it's called the parapet.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.990
That's the one that
soldiers will shoot over it.

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:12.840
The other one is called parados.

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:14.460
And that was actually
built slightly higher

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:16.297
than the parapet, and the reason for that

00:07:16.297 --> 00:07:20.310
was that if a soldier put
his head up over the parapet

00:07:20.310 --> 00:07:22.350
and if there wasn't any earth behind him,

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.383
then he would be --
you'd have the horizon.

00:07:24.383 --> 00:07:25.680
He'd be easily seen.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:27.180
Now, there's going to be an attack,

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:30.030
and all of these troops are
going to go over the parapet.

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:31.620
Why doesn't he just
climb over the parados?

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:32.550
There's nobody there.

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:36.180
He could simply run unimpeded
without any problems.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:37.230
He'd even get some cover

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:39.813
from the guys going over the parapet.

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:49.980
If you send your men forward
without any artillery support,

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:52.560
everybody is going to die.

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:54.180
They didn't send men over like this.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:58.770
The guidelines that the
army releases in early 1917

00:07:58.770 --> 00:08:01.050
is that when you advance,

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.600
you need to advance with the artillery,

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.230
and you have what's
called a creeping barrage.

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:11.310
And what a creeping barrage
was, was a line of shells,

00:08:11.310 --> 00:08:14.190
which would pummel the ground
in front of the infantry

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.710
and move forward at set times.

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:20.160
And they did that in order
to clear no man's land

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:23.060
of any defenders, so you
need to be rid of them,

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:25.170
and then moving into the trenches

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:26.670
to keep the defenders' heads down,

00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:27.930
which you desperately needed to do

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:30.450
if everyone wasn't going to
be machine-gunned to death.

00:08:30.450 --> 00:08:33.420
So, by this point, April 1917,

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:35.670
you're going to only go forward

00:08:35.670 --> 00:08:37.200
with a creeping barrage, generally.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:38.820
You're not going to do it any other way.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.470
And that's just absent from here.

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:43.590
Everybody is just rushing
forward headlessly.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:45.480
If they actually get to the German lines,

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:46.796
which they're clearly not going to do

00:08:46.796 --> 00:08:47.790
because they haven't got
any artillery support,

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:50.010
but what are they going
to do once they're there?

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:51.000
Where are the NCOs?

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:54.270
Where are the officers guiding
them, controlling the units,

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.095
doing their best to keep people together?

00:09:02.191 --> 00:09:04.860
The helmets that they used
were called Brodie helmets,

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:07.590
and they were above all designed

00:09:07.590 --> 00:09:09.690
to protect against shrapnel.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:11.790
Because if you think about the position

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:12.960
that these soldiers are in,

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:15.810
they're actually defended
very well on either side

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:17.160
by the trench itself.

00:09:17.160 --> 00:09:19.860
Trenches really were very
effective at protecting soldiers.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:21.720
It's only when soldiers leave the trenches

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:23.640
that they become extremely vulnerable.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:25.680
Where they are vulnerable, though,

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:27.360
is on the top of their heads.

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:28.997
And so that was why you had this kind of

00:09:28.997 --> 00:09:32.160
battle-bowler-type arrangement,
with the hardened steel

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:35.220
and the rim around it to
protect the head above all.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:37.200
That was why they were designed like this.

00:09:37.200 --> 00:09:38.771
I'd give it a four.

00:09:38.771 --> 00:09:43.713
[officers shouting]

00:09:43.713 --> 00:09:47.040
You hear them shouting in
German, "To arms, to arms."

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:48.540
And then this machine gun opens up.

00:09:48.540 --> 00:09:50.580
And that seems to me very, very plausible.

00:09:50.580 --> 00:09:52.200
And then they switch on a searchlight,

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.720
which was pretty common
on the eastern front.

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:55.980
You'd have searchlights
you could switch on

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:58.410
to illuminate the front of your defenses.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:01.320
This is representing the
so-called Christmas battles

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:04.080
of early 1917.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:07.560
And this is a unit called
the Latvian Riflemen.

00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:11.130
They were a nationalist
Latvian unit that were raised

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:13.440
and that served in the
imperial Russian army

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:16.170
operating up on the north
of the eastern front

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:17.460
in what today is Latvia.

00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:20.240
And this was a really marshy area,

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:24.150
so it wasn't possible to
dig trenches in this area.

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:26.434
This is the eastern front. This
is different from the west.

00:10:26.434 --> 00:10:28.603
When the Germans took it in 1915,

00:10:28.603 --> 00:10:33.600
what they did was they built a long wall

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:37.950
made of sand and wood,
which they fortified.

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:39.775
And you can see that really well.

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:51.150
The other aspect of this
scene that's authentic

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.300
is that when the Latvian rifles attacked

00:10:54.300 --> 00:10:55.710
this part of the German line,

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:58.080
they had to get through the barbed wire.

00:10:58.080 --> 00:10:58.920
You had to find a way

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.530
without blowing the barbed
wire up using artillery.

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:03.599
And what we're seeing here is

00:11:03.599 --> 00:11:06.180
what the British army would've
called a Bangalore torpedo,

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:07.770
which was like a pipe bomb.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:10.920
It was pushed underneath the wire,

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:15.390
and then it had a fuse going
back, and then you'd light it.

00:11:15.390 --> 00:11:17.370
And that's what these
two guys are doing here.

00:11:17.370 --> 00:11:21.390
They've got these hardened
metal plates in front of them

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:22.650
to try and protect them from bullets.

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.660
And it's their job to light the fuse,

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:27.043
which will then send off the wire.

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:36.690
You see this wave of riflemen coming out

00:11:36.690 --> 00:11:39.330
disguised in snow camouflage.

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:40.709
They would've been bedsheets

00:11:40.709 --> 00:11:43.860
or any white material that
they could've got hold of.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:45.750
The other thing that's
great about this clip

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:50.052
is it reminds us that the
winter could actually be

00:11:50.052 --> 00:11:51.960
a point of really huge activity.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:55.680
If you want to take an area
that's a marsh, though,

00:11:55.680 --> 00:11:58.620
actually you can't do
that for most of the year.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:00.300
The only point at which you can do it

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:02.490
is when the marsh freezes over.

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:05.160
And that's what happened in January 1917.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:08.250
And that was when these
Latvian troops went forward

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:09.630
and attacked this German line.

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:11.372
They could suddenly get over the marsh.

00:12:19.500 --> 00:12:21.390
What you see is the main character,

00:12:21.390 --> 00:12:22.223
he's dropping to the ground,

00:12:22.223 --> 00:12:24.600
he's taking cover behind a corpse

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:27.060
as the machine gun moves towards him.

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:31.950
And then you see a line
of soldiers falling,

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:35.010
one after the other, from right to left.

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:36.240
And it's clear that the machine gun

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:39.270
is traversing back away from him.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:41.430
And he leaps up, and he moves to the left

00:12:41.430 --> 00:12:42.840
away from the machine gun's fire.

00:12:42.840 --> 00:12:44.587
And this is what gives him the opportunity

00:12:44.587 --> 00:12:47.370
to throw a hand grenade
and destroy the gun.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:50.310
And it's a really neat way of showing

00:12:50.310 --> 00:12:51.540
how an experienced combat troop

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:55.440
would be very alert to what was going on,

00:12:55.440 --> 00:12:56.850
to where the machine gun was firing.

00:12:56.850 --> 00:13:00.600
And veteran troops did learn
ways how to deal with it.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:01.890
It's part of the learning process

00:13:01.890 --> 00:13:03.532
that goes on in the First World War.

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:13.350
It's just such a nice human detail

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:15.840
that they've managed to get
this far, right to the wall,

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:18.000
in part thanks to the snow
because the marsh is frozen,

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:20.790
but then they can't
easily get up this slope

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:22.170
because it's icy.

00:13:22.170 --> 00:13:25.110
And so they're using
their bayonets as ice axes

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:27.690
in order to carve some
sort of foothold in it.

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:29.370
You can see the desperation there.

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:31.470
And it's just this tiny little detail

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:34.200
that just makes this scene
so totally plausible.

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:35.288
I think this is fantastic.

00:13:35.288 --> 00:13:36.697
I give this a 10.

00:13:44.707 --> 00:13:48.000
So, this clip portrays
a fictional naval battle

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:50.670
between a Russian destroyer

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:54.960
and a German armored
cruiser in the Baltic Sea.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:56.065
What's hinted at in the clip

00:13:56.065 --> 00:13:59.070
but what maybe doesn't come
through as well as it could

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:03.300
is that these two ships are
of a massively different size.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:04.980
There's literally nothing
this Russian destroyer

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:08.520
could have done to hurt this cruiser.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:09.990
Some of them were equipped with torpedoes,

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:11.430
and that potentially could have done.

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:13.350
But as becomes clear in this film,

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:15.630
this particular ship
is equipped with mines.

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:17.372
So there really isn't any defense

00:14:17.372 --> 00:14:20.489
that this ship has got
against the Friedrich Carl.

00:14:24.331 --> 00:14:26.310
The cruiser has multiple guns,

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:28.770
but the biggest on it, 21 centimeters.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:33.450
If those 21 centimeters
hit the Russian destroyer,

00:14:33.450 --> 00:14:35.010
it's going to sink, or at the very least

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:37.500
it's going to have its
infrastructure wiped off it.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:39.484
It's going to be a huge amount of damage.

00:14:46.050 --> 00:14:49.710
Huge German shell hits this gun tower,

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:52.200
and it's not even that
the gun tower goes down.

00:14:52.200 --> 00:14:54.600
The gun is still in working order.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:57.175
There's nothing realistic
about this at all.

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:06.510
You'd be very unlikely to
have combat this close either.

00:15:06.510 --> 00:15:09.993
The German armored
cruiser would've destroyed

00:15:09.993 --> 00:15:13.002
the Russian ship before
it got anywhere near it.

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:19.560
It is true that the Friedrich
Carl was sunk by a mine.

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:22.740
If a German ship hit a mine,
you would get an explosion,

00:15:22.740 --> 00:15:25.020
but down near the waterline.

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:27.360
There would be no reason
to have this huge explosion

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:30.900
that they've produced on
the bridge or on the deck.

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:33.630
That doesn't make any sense.

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:36.900
There was an explosion, but
it sank really, really slowly.

00:15:36.900 --> 00:15:38.580
It didn't explode in flames.

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:40.355
And in the end,

00:15:40.355 --> 00:15:43.050
the Germans managed to
rescue all but eight people.

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:45.547
This idea that there was a
fight between a destroyer

00:15:45.547 --> 00:15:49.050
and the armed cruiser,
that's not the case.

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:50.010
I'll give it a two.

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:52.110
The battle itself is pure make-believe.

00:15:52.110 --> 00:15:53.310
Thanks so much for watching.

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:55.350
If you want to see more
videos in this series,

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:56.550
click on the link above.

